  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">CONSERVING PRIORITY IBAS FOR AQUATIC MIG</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">This next phase aims to improve the conservation of priority areas for aquatic migratory bird populations in coastal Ecuador at Ecuasal Saltlakes (1300 hectares), Corazn and Fragata Wildlife Refuge (2800 hectares) and Canal of Jambel in the Gulf of Guayaquil (55000 hectares). Ecuasal sustains habitat for 5 to10 percent of the biogeographic population of Wilson&apos;s Phalarope; Corazn and Fragata Wildlife Refuge accounts for 3 to 5 percent of biogeographic population of Wilson&apos;s Plover (beldingii) and 1percent of Whimbrel (rufiventris); and the Canal of Jambeli accounts for significant percentages of the biogeographic populations for: Wilson&apos;s Plover (beldingi) (13.3 percent), Semipalmated Sandpiper (10 percent), Whimbrel (rufiventris) (8.75 percent), Semipalmated Plover (6.7 percent) and American Oystercatcher (pitanay) (2.5 percent) biogeographic populations. The main goal of this project is to implement Ecuadors Migratory Shorebirds Action Plan, which was officially endorsed through a ministerial resolution in July 2021. Site-specific actions at Ecuasal Saltlakes include strengthening ongoing programs for habitat management, research and monitoring and environmental education. At Corazn and Fragata Wildlife Refuge, actions include updating their management plan as a mechanism to secure good governance and commitments with shrimp farmers to reduce aquatic contamination. At Canal of Jambel, grantees will deliver participatory workshops with stakeholders to implement an Integrated Coastal Management Plan endorsed by Naranjal and Balao Municipalities. This will set limits to shrimp farm expansions, reduce aquatic contamination, and identify priority conservation actions.</narrative>
  </description>
